  "context": [
    "The Punch launched in January 2021 as Tata's micro-SUV, sitting below the Nexon while riding on the same ALFA platform that underpins the Altroz. Its raised ground clearance and boxy, SUV-styled body tapped directly into the Indian market's growing preference for SUV silhouettes even at hatchback-adjacent prices, and it quickly became one of the best-selling vehicles in its segment, a volume position that has kept used-market demand consistently strong.",
    "A 5-star Global NCAP rating, achieved without structural reinforcement specifically for the crash test, reinforced Tata's safety-focused brand positioning that the Altroz had already established, and resale buyers in this segment increasingly treat that rating as a genuine purchase factor rather than a marketing footnote. The Punch has carried only a 1.2-litre naturally aspirated petrol throughout its ICE lifecycle, with CNG (iCNG) added in 2023 to widen its appeal to cost-conscious buyers.",
    "Tata launched a separate battery-electric Punch EV in 2024 on a dedicated platform, which is a distinct product line with its own depreciation and battery-health considerations rather than a variant of the ICE Punch; the petrol and CNG Punch covered here continues to account for the large majority of used-market volume and listings.",
    "Because the Punch competes directly with larger-feeling compact SUVs on styling while costing closer to a premium hatchback, it has drawn a wide first-owner base across both urban and smaller-town buyers, and that broad demand base is the main reason its resale curve sits slightly ahead of the compact-SUV segment average rather than behind it."
  ],
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the resale value of a Tata Punch in India?",
      "a": "A well-maintained petrol Punch retains roughly 76% of its ex-showroom value after two years and 68% after three years, as of September 2026. A 2023 petrol Adventure with 25,000-50,000 km currently trades in a fair-market band of roughly ₹5.6-6.0 lakh."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does the Punch CNG hold value better than the petrol version?",
      "a": "In CNG-dense markets such as Ahmedabad and Delhi NCR, CNG Punch units trade close to or slightly above equivalent petrol cars of the same age, reflecting the running-cost advantage used buyers specifically look for."
    },
    {
      "q": "How much does a Tata Punch depreciate per year?",
      "a": "The Punch loses roughly 14% in year one and 8% in year two, then declines 6-7% annually through years three to five, reaching about 56% retention at the five-year mark, a few points ahead of the compact-SUV segment average."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is the Punch EV the same as the petrol Punch for resale purposes?",
      "a": "No, the Punch EV runs on a separate battery-electric platform launched in 2024 and should be assessed independently, since its depreciation pattern is driven by battery health and EV-specific demand rather than the ICE engine and fuel-type factors covered here."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which city gives the best resale price for a Tata Punch?",
      "a": "Ahmedabad currently trades at roughly +3% above the national mid thanks to strong CNG demand, while Kolkata trades slightly softer at around -1%."
    }
  ],
